    "recommendations": "Reducing the density of heavy brush and concentrated brush areas on the property, particularly within the immediate vicinity of the structure, could meaningfully improve defensible space and reduce fuel continuity across the site. Where shrubs and mixed vegetation are growing adjacent to or in close proximity to the structure, increasing horizontal separation between individual plants and between vegetation and the structure would support suppression operations and reduce direct fuel exposure to the building. Managing vegetation to create irregular spacing rather than continuous cover may reduce the opportunity for fire to travel uninterrupted across the property. Given that access to the property relies on a single route through a brushland interface, maintaining that access road clear of encroaching shrubs and overhanging vegetation along its full length is a practical priority, as it would support both evacuation and firefighter access. Preserving any existing open terrain on the property as a natural fuel break, and avoiding future introduction of additional vegetation in those areas, could further support long-term wildfire resilience."
